{:schema 1
 :date "2026-08-14"
 :receipt/type :synthesis
 :question "Six measurement receipts found per-row costs worth 0.15% to 6%. The gap to Neo4j is 9.7x. Where is it?"
 :answer "In what a row costs, uniformly. Not in any one query's plan or strategy."

 :the-evidence
 {:paired-p50-ratio-vs-neo4j {:ic07 0.995 :ic08 1.855 :ic02 9.710 :ic09 8.641}
  :rows-the-engine-materialises
  {:ic02 2702  :note-ic02 "sum of bindings-out per step, 882 + 910 + 910 (ic02-diag)"
   :ic09 230321 :note-ic09 "882 + 55,335 + 87,052 + 87,052 (ic02-diag's ic09-steps)"
   :ic07 "small -- the likes relation is 809 rows"
   :ic08 "small -- replyOf is 4,355 rows"}
  :relation-sizes {:knows 361246 :hasCreator 7355 :message-creationDate 7355 :replyOf 4355 :like-message 809}}

 :the-reading
 "IC02 and IC09 differ by 85x in rows materialised and by almost nothing in
  ratio: 9.710 against 8.641. Two row-dominated queries landing on the same
  ratio is what a constant per-row factor looks like -- both engines pay per
  row, and the quotient is the ratio of their per-row costs.

  IC07 and IC08 sit near parity because they touch few rows, so per-query fixed
  costs dominate and the per-row difference has nothing to multiply.

  This also explains why six receipts of micro-measurement produced so little:
  the quad map is 6%, the intermediate set 0.15%, `map vector` in unify 26% of
  one sub-term. Removing all of them together cannot turn 9x into 1x, because
  they are pieces of the same constant factor rather than a mistake sitting on
  top of it."

 :what-a-row-costs-here
 {:index-read 0.4 :unit "ns/row, estimate-cardinality on knows"
  :plus-quad-maps-and-visible 131.3
  :plus-hashing-into-a-set 588.6
  :plus-unify-and-projection 2105.1
  :note "Each layer measured over the same 361,246-row relation on 2026-08-14.
         A row costs about 2 microseconds to move through the engine and about
         0.4 nanoseconds to find."}

 :what-would-actually-move-it
 "Not an optimisation. The per-row cost is the binding representation (a
  persistent map extended by `assoc` per variable per row), the quad
  materialisation ({:s :p :o} per candidate), and set semantics at every step.
  Those are the engine's data model, and they are also what makes it portable,
  immutable and content-addressable. Changing any of them is a design decision
  with consequences well outside query latency, and this receipt does not make
  one -- it says only that no amount of profiling inside the current
  representation will find a 9x."

 :what-this-does-not-claim
 ["Not that the engine is badly built. IC07 at 0.995 against embedded Neo4j says
   the plan, the index routing and the join are sound; what differs is the cost
   of a row once found."
  "Not that 9x is fixed. It says the remaining 9x is in the representation, so
   the next experiment worth running is a representation experiment, not another
   profile."
  "Two of the four ratios (IC02, IC09) support the constant-factor reading; two
   (IC07, IC08) are consistent with it but too small to test it. Four points is
   four points."]

 :the-honest-summary-of-six-iterations
 "Measured: planner estimation (fixed, 7,470x on the probe, IC07 to parity),
  unify allocation (landed, 2-5% on IC02), quad maps (6%, not worth it), the
  intermediate set (0.15%, not worth it). Two receipts were wrong and corrected
  in place. What was learned that survives is the shape of the remaining gap,
  and that it is not reachable from here."}
